\title{\textbf{Coinductive Symmetric Homomorphism Reinforcement Learning} \\
A New Foundation Where Optimality Is Pure Structure}

\author{Ricardo Corral-Corral}
\date{December 13, 2025}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We completely redefine optimal sequential decision making. Traditionally, an optimal agent is one that maximizes the expected sum of discounted rewards [1]. We argue this is a flawed proxy. Instead of approximating value functions (the dominant TD-learning paradigm [1]), instead of chasing gradients (as in Policy Gradient methods [2]), instead of adding entropy bonuses (the MaxEnt framework used in Soft Actor-Critic [3]) we demand one thing and one thing only: that the ranking of actions in any state be a perfect symmetric reflection of the ranking of the infinite futures those actions produce not just now, but at every single future moment, forever. This condition is formalized as a coinductive symmetric homomorphism [5]. It is stronger than Bayes-optimality [4]. It is stronger than Pareto-optimality. And when it holds, the agent is provably optimal in the strongest possible sense while automatically solving constraints, exploration, and long-term credit assignment. The entire theory is implemented and machine-verified in Agda, with a complete, concise coinductive optimality proof.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction: The Scalar Trap}

Optimal sequential decision making is the study of how an agent should act in an environment to maximize a signal of success. The standard definition, which has driven the field of Reinforcement Learning (RL) for decades, asserts that an optimal policy $\pi^*$ is one that maximizes the expected cumulative discounted reward:

\[
J(\pi) = \mathbb{E}_{\tau \sim \pi} \left[ \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \gamma^t r_t \right]
\]

While mathematically convenient, this definition rests on a fragile assumption: that the rich, structural complexity of "intelligent behavior" can be perfectly compressed into a single scalar number. This scalar compression introduces fundamental artifacts that plague modern AI:

\begin{itemize}
\item The Discount Factor ($\gamma$): An arbitrary parameter required for convergence, forcing agents to artificially devalue the future.
\item Value Aliasing: Two vastly different futures (e.g., "win then die" vs "struggle then thrive") may sum to the same number, confusing the optimizer.
\item Instability: Learning algorithms like Q-learning rely on bootstrapping these scalar estimates, leading to the deadly triad of divergence.
\end{itemize}

We propose a fundamental shift. Optimality is not about summing numbers. It is about preserving structure. We define optimality not as a maximum, but as a symmetry.

\section{The Fundamental Revelation}

Stop for a moment and consider what it really means to understand a task. You are in a maze. You have five actions: up, down, left, right, wait. Some lead to cheese. Some lead to shock. One is blocked by a wall. Traditional RL says: assign a number to each action, maximize the number.

We say something far more powerful:

\begin{quote}
An agent understands the maze perfectly when it can look at any permutation of the five actions and see that the permutation of the infinite futures they produce is exactly the same permutation not just in total value, but step by step, forever.
\end{quote}

That is the entire theory. If this symmetry holds, the agent is optimal. If it does not hold, learning is nothing more than the process of making the symmetry truer. No gradients. No Bellman equation. No regret bounds. Just symmetry.

\section{The True Definition: A Mirror That Never Breaks}

Before we write any code, let us state the coinductive symmetric homomorphism mathematically. The definition has three components: the \emph{domain} (actions with their ranking), the \emph{codomain} (infinite outcome streams with their dominance order), and the \emph{homomorphism} (the map that preserves structure).

\subsection{The Domain: Actions and Rankings}

Let $\mathcal{A}$ be the finite set of actions available to the agent. In any state $s$, the agent maintains a \textbf{total preorder} $\le_{\text{rank}}$ on $\mathcal{A}$:
\[
a \le_{\text{rank}} b \quad \text{means ``action } b \text{ is at least as good as action } a \text{''}
\]
This ranking is what the agent \emph{believes} about the relative quality of actions. The question is: when is this belief \emph{correct}?

\subsection{The Codomain: Infinite Outcome Streams}

Let $\mathcal{O}$ be the set of infinite outcome streams---sequences of rewards (or state-reward pairs) extending forever into the future. We equip $\mathcal{O}$ with a \textbf{coinductive dominance order} $\le_s$, defined recursively:
\[
x \le_s y \;\;\Longleftrightarrow\;\; \text{head}(x) \le_r \text{head}(y) \;\wedge\; \text{tail}(x) \le_s \text{tail}(y)
\]
where $\le_r$ is the order on rewards, $\text{head}(x)$ is the first element of stream $x$, and $\text{tail}(x)$ is the remaining infinite stream after the first element.

\begin{remark}
This is \emph{coinductive}: the definition refers to itself on the tails. Unlike induction (which builds up from base cases), coinduction unfolds forever. The relation $x \le_s y$ holds if and only if at \emph{every} time step $t = 0, 1, 2, \ldots$, the $t$-th element of $x$ is $\le_r$ the $t$-th element of $y$.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The Homomorphism: Structure Preservation}

Now we define the core concept. Let $h : \mathcal{A} \to \mathcal{O}$ be the \textbf{action-value map}: for each action $a$, the stream $h(a)$ represents the infinite sequence of rewards obtained by taking action $a$ and then acting optimally forever.

\begin{definition}[Coinductive Symmetric Homomorphism]
The map $h$ is a \textbf{coinductive symmetric homomorphism} if it satisfies:

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Order Preservation:} For all actions $a, b \in \mathcal{A}$,
\[
a \le_{\text{rank}} b \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; h(a) \le_s h(b)
\]
The ranking of actions is \emph{exactly mirrored} by the dominance of their infinite futures.

\item \textbf{Coinductive Unfolding:} The preservation holds not just at time $t=0$, but at \emph{every future time step}. Formally, if we denote $h_t$ as the action-value map at time $t$, then for all $t \ge 0$:
\[
a \le_{\text{rank}} b \;\;\Longrightarrow\;\; \text{head}(h_t(a)) \le_r \text{head}(h_t(b)) \;\wedge\; h_{t+1}(a') \le_s h_{t+1}(b')
\]
where $a'$ and $b'$ are the ``continuation actions'' after the first step.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\subsection{The Symmetry Condition}

The ``symmetric'' in coinductive symmetric homomorphism refers to \textbf{equivariance} under relabeling. Let $\sigma \in \text{Perm}(\mathcal{A})$ be any permutation of actions, and let $\tau \in \text{Perm}(\mathcal{O})$ be the induced permutation of outcome streams. The homomorphism commutes with these permutations:
\[
h \circ \sigma = \tau \circ h
\]
This says: if we relabel the actions by $\sigma$, the outcome streams are relabeled by the corresponding $\tau$. The structure is preserved under all possible renamings.

\subsection{The Infinite Tower of Diagrams}

Putting it all together, the coinductive symmetric homomorphism is an \textbf{infinite tower of commuting diagrams}---one for each time step, forever:

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\begin{tikzpicture}[
    node distance=3cm and 5.5cm,
    obj/.style={draw,rounded corners,minimum height=2.5em,minimum width=5.5em,align=center,font=\small},
    arr/.style={->,thick,>=Stealth,shorten >=3pt,shorten <=3pt}
]
% Top row: time t
\node[obj] (A1) {$\mathcal{A}$};
\node[obj,right=of A1] (S1) {$\mathcal{O}$};
\draw[arr] (A1) -- node[above] {$h_t$} (S1);

% Self-loops on left and right sides of nodes (not top)
\draw[arr] (A1) to[out=150,in=210,looseness=5] node[left] {$\sigma$} (A1);
\draw[arr] (S1) to[out=330,in=30,looseness=5] node[right] {$\tau$} (S1);

% Bottom row: time t+1
\node[obj,below=of A1] (A2) {$\mathcal{A}$};
\node[obj,right=of A2] (S2) {$\mathcal{O}$};
\draw[arr] (A2) -- node[above] {$h_{t+1}$} (S2);

% Vertical arrows (tail operations)
\draw[arr,dashed] (A1) -- node[right,font=\footnotesize] {tail} (A2);
\draw[arr,dashed] (S1) -- node[left,font=\footnotesize] {tail} (S2);

% Ellipsis indicating continuation
\node[below=0.6cm of A2] {$\vdots$};
\node[below=0.6cm of S2] {$\vdots$};

